# Simple, Robust, and Going More Efficient: Recent Advance on Electron Transport Layer‐Free Perovskite Solar Cells

## abstract
Perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have shown great potential for photovoltaic applications with their unprecedented power conversion efficiency advancement. Such devices generally have a complex structure design with high temperature processed TiO2 as the electron transport layer (ETL). Further careful design of device configuration to fully tap the potentials of perovskite materials is expected. Particularly, for the practical application of PSCs, it is crucial to simplify their device structures thus the associated manufacturing process and cost while maintaining their efficiency to be comparable with the conventional devices. But how simple is simple? ETL-free PSCs promise the simplest structured, thus simple manufacturing processes and low cost large area PSCs in practical applications. They can also help the further exploration of the great potential of perovskite materials and understanding the working principle of PSCs. Within this review, the evolution of the PSC is outlined by discussing the recent advances in the simplification of device configuration and processes for cost effective, highly efficient, and robust PSCs, with a focus on ETLfree PSCs. Their advancement, key issues, working mechanism, existing problems, and future performance enhancements. This review aims to promote the future development of low cost and robust ETL-free PSCs toward more efficient power output.

Like Huang and Ziyi Ge\*
Perovskite solar cells (PSCs) have shown great potential for photovoltaic applications with their unprecedented power conversion efficiency advancement. Such devices generally have a complex structure design with high temperature processed $\bar{\mathsf{T i O}}_{2}$ as the electron transport layer (ETL). Further careful design of device configuration to fully tap the potentials of perovskite materials is expected. Particularly, for the practical application of PSCs, it is crucial to simplify their device structures thus the associated manufacturing process and cost while maintaining their efficiency to be comparable with the conventional devices. But how simple is simple? ETL-free PSCs promise the simplest structured, thus simple manufacturing processes and low cost large area PSCs in practical applications. They can also help the further exploration of the great potential of perovskite materials and understanding the working principle of PSCs. Within this review, the evolution of the PSC is outlined by discussing the recent advances in the simplification of device configuration and processes for cost effective, highly efficient, and robust PSCs, with a focus on ETLfree PSCs. Their advancement, key issues, working mechanism, existing problems, and future performance enhancements. This review aims to promote the future development of low cost and robust ETL-free PSCs toward more efficient power output.
## 1. Introduction
Thin-film solar cells with organic and inorganic metal halide perovskite as the light absorb layer have recently been the focus subject of intensive research and the recent record efficiency has been up to $23.7\%$ .[1–12] Most of the high efficient devices utilize mesoporous and/or compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ as electron transport layer (ETL), which needs high processing temperatures.[1–3,5–7,9,11,13,14] To reduce thermal budget and complexity of fabrication, planar structures have been proposed, with metal oxides such as $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , $z\mathrm{nO}$ , and $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ .[5,15–18] Unfortunately, planar devices based on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and $\mathsf{Z n O}$ suffer from severe hysteresis and ETL/perovskite interface instability due to oxygen vacancies induced ultraviolet photocatalytic degradation and proton reaction.[19–24] Soon, initially proposed high-efficient perovskite solar cells (PSCs) inherited the structure of solid-state dyesensitized solar cells and have been rapidly extended to several types of p–i–n, p–i, and i–n and even metal–semiconductor– metal (MSM) junction solar cells.[25–36] For most high efficient PSCs, they commonly contain both ETL and hole-transporting layer (HTL) to efficiently collect one kind of charge while block the other kind to avoid carrier recombination thus high performance can be ensured.[37–41] The widely used material for the ETL is $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ that needs a high temperature sintering process which consumes extra energy and limits the scalability of PSCs.[9] To circumvent this drawback, many attempts were made to explore low temperature solution processed electron selective materials, such as $z\mathrm{nO}$ , $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{SnO}_{4}$ , $\mathrm{\WO}_{3}$ , and fullerene derivatives.[13] However, their synthesis and deposition still requires an additional step and the stability of the resulted device is unsatisfactory. Because intrinsic metal oxides, such as $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , usually are not conductive as the bandgap is too wide without doping induced free carriers. The reason why they can be semiconductive and used as the electron/hole transport layer is because of intentional or unintentional doping, such as nonstoichiometric composition with oxygen vacancy. Oxygen vacancy is necessary for the conductivity of metal oxides as an ETL, but the light induced catalytic degradation caused by oxygen vacancies at the perovskite/ETL is disadvantageous to the stability of the device.[21] As a direct solution to these problems, the elimination of the ETL can further simplify the device configuration and bring down the fabrication cost.[32,42]
In view of a large dielectric constant and thus a small exciton binding energy of perovskite itself, electron–hole pairs (only a very small portion of excitons) that are generated in perovskite following light absorption can be readily separated after thermalization. Thus, the existence of the p–i or i–n heterojunction within the widely studied device with a conventional p–i–n structure is not mainly and predominately to dissociate excitons.[43,44] However, for the closely followed charge separation process, heterojunction may be very important as it can occur through two major reactions: 1) injection of photogenerated electrons into ETL such as $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ and/or 2) injection of holes into a HTL such as spiro-MeOTAD.[45,46] For both electrons and holes injection or extraction, p–i–n or $\mathrm{{\n-i-p}}$ structure as depicted in Figure 1a is widely adopted.
## 2. Working Principle of PSCs from a Heterojunction Point of View
In the p–i–n or n–i–p device (Figure  1a), perovskite film (the i layer) is responsible for absorbing light and generating carriers. For ETL, it can extract the electrons from i layer by electron flow or injection with energetic favorable energy level alignment at the n–i interface. ETL with wide bandgap and deeper valence band edge (VB) also acts as hole-blocking layer by forming energy barrier for hole injection at the i–n interface, which is needed to prevent holes generated in the perovskite layer from reaching the fluorine-doped tin oxide (FTO) electrode. While, the presence of pinholes in the compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer deteriorates the performance of PSCs as they would shortcircuit the cell. This is also the view of point that people generally hold in the early stages of PSCs research. Thus, many work at that time emphasized the optimization of the ETL with respect of its density, regularity, conductivity, and energy level alignment with perovskite at the n–i interface to promote the interface charge transfer/injection and selective collection capacity to avoid interface charge accumulation and recombination, which helps to eliminate the S-shape $J{-}V$ curves and hysteresis, thereby increasing device efficiency and stability. For HTL, it can extract the holes from i layer by hole flow or injection with energetic favorable energy level alignment at the $\mathrm{i-p}$ interface. HTL with wide bandgap and shallower conduction band edge (CB) also acts as electron-blocking layer by forming energy barrier for electron injection at the $\mathrm{i-p}$ interface, which is critical to prevent electrons formed in the perovskite layer from reaching the metal cathode. Some studies have shown that the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of PSCs depends on the splitting degree between Fermi level of perovskite and HTL. So, many work addressing the mobility, conductivity, and energy level alignment with perovskite at the i–p interface to promote the interface charge collection thus the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and power conversion efficiency (PCE) are reported. Etgar and co-workers confirmed the electronic structure of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}/$ perovskite junctions in PSCs with Kelvin probe force microscopy (KPFM, Figure 1b,c).[47] At the interface between $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ and nanocrystalline $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (Figure 1d), a depletion region of ${\approx}45$ nm was derived from the potential profiles measured. The band diagrams at the interfaces, as shown in Figure 1e, were estimated from the measured potential profile. Guerrero et  al. studied the electrical field profile and doping within the planar PSCs.[48] To establish the operating energyband diagram of the device, electrical field distribution is experimentally determined using capacitance-voltage and KPFM (Figure 1f,g). Depletion width in equilibrium within the perovskite bulk has an extent about $300\ \mathrm{nm}$ (about half of the layer thickness), leaving a significant neutral zone toward the anode contact. Charge collection properties are then accessible relying on the relative weight that diffusion and drift have as carrier transport driven forces.[49]
