Although the ETL/perovskite heterojunction is critical for carrier extraction and device performance, on the one hand, more detailed photoelectron physics studies have shown that perovskite as bipolar semiconductors have large and balanced electron/hole carrier mobility and diffusion length, being capable of transporting both electrons and holes efficiently and simultaneously.[50,51] This means that perovskite itself or some portion of perovskite light absorption layer can serve as both an ETL and an HTL at the same time. Thus, ETL-free or HTL-free device is theoretically possible.[52]
On the other hand, the widely used high temperature processed $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ based ETL encountered process compatibility issues in flexible application and stability issues due to its UV photocatalytic degradation effect. The high temperature sintering process consumes extra energy and hinders the scalability of PSCs. As a direct solution to these problems, the elimination of the ETL may further simplify the device fabrication and bring down the cost. Coupled with the desire to remove the HTL to reduce the manufacturing cost of the device, HTL-free PSCs were proposed prior to the ETL-free counterpart. In this respect, Han et al. reported very encouraging work that promises bright future of commercial applications of fully printed PSCs based on low-cost HTL-free and C electrode material scheme without usage of mostly expensive but unstable organic semiconductor materials as HTL.[29] Also, increasing efficiency is reported based on HTL-free PSCs and the recent efficiency record has been achieved up to $20\%$ , suggesting that HTL is not indispensable for efficient devices (unless to obtain a record efficiency).[53] The remove of ETL can further simplify the device manufacturing process and reduce manufacturing cost while it does not sacrifice the efficiency of the device too much.
Interestingly, though ETL-free device has a symmetrical device structure and same bipolar charge transport properties of perovskite like HTL-free device, all current ETL-free PSCs, unfortunately, suffer from poor PCEs compared to that of the HTL-free device.[32] Also, the operation mechanism understanding of ETL-free device is much lagging behind.[54,55] So far, there have been gradually growing reports on ETL-free devices.
## 3. The Role of ETL and Definition of ETL-Free PSCs
## 3.1. The Role of ETL
Before revealing the role of ETL and introducing the concept of the ETL-free PSCs, it is necessary to specify the exact meaning of ETL primarily. ETL is consisting of electron transport material that can extract negative charged carriers (electrons) from absorb layer and transmit them to the electron collection electrode. Zhan and co-workers pointed that the ETL should have a comparable or a slightly lower lowest unoccupied molecular orbital (LUMO) level relative to that of the perovskite active materials, combining a strong electron-accepting property to facilitate electron selection.[13] Semiconductor materials (usually n-type semiconductors) with high electron affinity and ion potential are usually used as electron transport materials. The basic function of ETL is to form an electron selective contact with perovskite absorb layer, improve the extraction efficiency of photogenerated electrons, and effectively prevent the hole from migrating toward the cathode. In principle, the major benefits of an ETL are selectively extracting the electrons from the perovskite layer to reduce charge accumulation, recombination, and transporting electrons in a more efficient way compared to the perovskite where holes also exist. Mahmood et al. proposed that the most important characteristic of an ETL is that it must satisfy band alignment with the perovskite layer, i.e., it should have LUMO and highest occupied molecular orbital higher than the perovskite active layer as shown in Figure  1a with staggered energy level alignment.[55b] In addition, ideal ETL should be n-type semiconductor, preferably unipolar n-type semiconductor to transport electron only and block hole to prevent carrier recombination, regardless of their specific composition, stoichiometric ratio, and organic or inorganic nature. In the device with $\mathrm{{\underline{{n-i-p}}}}$ structure, ETL as window layer should also has a wide bandgap to ensure the transmission of all the light that can be absorbed by perovskite absorb layer. After modification of surface with ligands, ETL can also improve device stability and eliminate hysteresis effect by inhibiting carrier recombination at the interface.
According to the definition and characteristic of an ETL, the widely reported ETL materials can be divided into at least the following categories: 1) n-type metal oxides semiconductor, such as binary $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , $z\mathrm{nO}$ , $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ , $\ensuremath{\mathbb{W}}\ensuremath{\operatorname{O}_{3}}$ , $\mathsf{N b}_{2}\mathsf{O}_{5}$ , $\mathrm{In}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ ; ternary $\mathrm{SrTiO}_{3}$ , $\ensuremath{\mathrm{MnZnO_{3}}}$ , ${\mathrm{BsSnO}}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{Zn}_{2}\mathrm{SnO}_{4}$ ; quaternary ${\mathrm{Pb}}Z{\mathrm{rTiO}}_{3}$ ; and so on.[13] 2) n-type metal sulfide/selenide semiconductor, such as CdSe, ZnS quantum dot, $\mathrm{TiS}_{2}$ , $\mathsf{M o S}_{2}$ , ${\sf S b}_{2}{\sf S}_{3}$ . 3) n-type organic semiconductors, such as fullerenes or nonfullerenes, and their derivatives.[13] It must be pointed out that n-type organic–inorganic hybrid perovskite with appropriate energy levels and wide bandgap can also be used as electron transport layer material, although no devices using perovskite as ETL have been reported so far to our best knowledge. While, metal oxide or organic material (especially nonconjugate one) with too much large bandgap (for example, $3~\mathrm{eV}$ and mismatched CB levels with perovskite are not seen as ETL, such as $\mathtt{M g O}$ , ${\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ , $\mathrm{SiO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{HfO}_{2}$ , $\mathrm{ZrO}_{2}$ , which are usually used as interface modification layer or defect passivation layer with a thickness of about several nanometers that allows electrons to travel through in a quantum tunneling manner.[13] With their insulating nature, common organic interface modification layers (thickness of several nanometers to tens of nanometers), such as bathocuproine (BCP), polyethylenimine (PEIE), polyetherimide (PEI), poly[(9,9- bis( $3^{\prime}.$ -(N,N-dimethylamino)propyl)-2,7-fluorene)-alt-2,7-(9,9– dioctylfluorene)] (PFN), and their derivatives, are not regarded as ETL too. As for BCP, its exceptions and unique roles will be discussed in detail later. Due to the obvious tunneling effect, the self-assembled monolayer (SAM, thickness of about several angstroms) at the crossing boundary, either consists of conjugated or nonconjugated organic molecules, should not be seen as ETL.
Holding the point that a pinhole-free and uniform holeblocking layer is essentially necessary for highly efficient PSCs, in earlier studies (before 2015), many groups were committed to enhancing the density of compact layers (mainly $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ) in the early studies, ETL-free devices with poor performance were usually regarded as control device (Figure S1a,b, Supporting Information).[56–60] In Table 1, several examples of photovoltaic parameters of ETL-free PSCs as control device in the reference are listed. A typical work is conducted by Wu et  al., they revealed the effect of pinholes in compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layers on the device performance (Figure S1c–f, Supporting Information).[58] They found that $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ compact layers derived from atomic layer deposition (ALD) contain less pinholes than layers obtained by spin coating and spray pyrolysis methods, thus the ALD-based $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer functions as a vigorous hole-blocking layer in PSCs as it offers a large shunt resistance which enables a high PCE of $12.56\%$ . It is worth mentioning that as a control device to illustrate the significant effect of optimization of the ETL on device performance improvement, the performance of ETLfree devices may not be optimized in these works. Particularly, in the early stages of PSCs research, the growth process of the perovskite film was not well optimized as they are today, as evidenced by the hard-to-get PCE of $12.56\%$ based on ALD– $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer with extremely complex and expensive preparation methods at that time.
After 2015, with the optimization of the preparation process and the improvement of the quality of the perovskite film, ETL-free devices with much higher effciencies gradually raised, even though they were still used as control device to confirm the effectiveness of different ETL materials in the literature. For example, Miyasaka and co-workers reported efficient and environmentally stable PSCs based on $\mathsf{Z n O}$ ETL.[59] The thickness of $z\mathrm{nO}$ layer greatly affects the photovoltaic performance and a highest PCE of up to $13.9\%$ was demonstrated. While, for the device without an ETL, a PCE as high as $8.3\%$ was archived (Figure S1g, Supporting Information). In the work by Wang and co-workers, ETL-free device with PCE of $12.89\%$ was demonstrated, while the PCE of the aminosubstituted perylene diimide (N-PDI)/FTO based device gave a highest PCE of $17.66\%$ (Figure S1h, Supporting Information).[62] Liu and co-workers reported high-efficiency flexible PSCs using solid-state ionic-liquids as effective ETL which presents a highest PCE of $16.09\%$ , while the device without ETL gives a highest PCE of $12.50\%$ .[61] Masood et  al. studied the effect of film thickness of dip-coated compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layers on the performance of mesoscopic PSCs.[67] They found that very thin compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layers leads to an $^{\mathfrak{a}}\mathrm{S}$ -shaped” $J{-}V$ curves, deteriorating the device performance (Figure S2a, Supporting Information). The highest PCE of the ETL-free PSC was $6.3\%$ , when inserting a compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer the PCE was promoted up to $9.0\%$ . Huang and co-workers reported efficient PSCs with annealing-free $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ as ETL.[71] The planar PSC based on the optimized $\mathrm{Cr}_{2}\mathrm{O}_{3}$ ETL gains a highest PCE of $16.23\%$ , which is higher than that of the ETL-free device (Figure S2b, Supporting Information). In the recent work by Qi et al., a highest PCE of $18.5\%$ is achieved using anatase $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ as ETL, while the device is not stable.[68] With an amorphous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ compact layer, the devices show a enhanced lifetime but a poorer PCE and more serious hysteresis (Figure S3c, Supporting Information). The device without any $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ as ETL gives a much lower PCE of about $13.5\%$ but much stability was also demonstrated.
As for the role of ETL in device, in the earlier work by MoraSero and co-workers, the effect of electron- and hole-selective contacts on the final $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ solar cells performance has been systematically studied by impedance spectroscopy (Figure 2).[37] Generally, selective contacts play three major roles: transport resistance at the selector layer, charge-transfer rate at the interface, and the selective contact interface recombination. It has been revealed that both electron- and hole-selective contacts greatly affect the device performance by governing the recombination rate and controlling the recombination resistance. The selective contacts increase the cell series resistance, but this deleterious effect is then compensated for by the beneficial reduction in recombination rate. Impedance spectroscopy suggests that both selective contacts can improve the fill factor (FF), while the hole-selective contact mainly contributes to the high $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ (Figure 2b).
More detailed comparative studies were conducted by Xiao et  al. and Nazeeruddin and co-workers.[41,70] Using the potential profiling, Xiao et al. compared three types of cells made of different ETLs but otherwise having an identical device structure (Figure  3a).[70] The results reveal two major potential drops (electric fields) at the ETL/perovskite and perovskite/ HTL interfaces. The electric-field ratio between the ETL/perovskite and perovskite/HTL interfaces increased as follows: FTO $<\mathsf{S n O}_{2}-\mathsf{E T L}<\mathsf{S n O}_{2}+\mathsf{S A M}$ $\mathrm{\DeltaC_{60}}$ self-assembled monolayer). The increase of the FF from the FTO to $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ -ETL devices may result from the reduced voltage loss at the perovskite/HTL back interface and the improved $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ from the prevention of hole recombination at the ETL/perovskite front interface. The further improvements with adding a SAM is a result of the defect passivation at the ETL/perovskite interface, and thus, improvement of the junction quality (Figure 3b). Their study demonstrates the great effect of ETL and interfacial layer on the interface energy level alignment, the junction quality thus the final device performance. Different form their view point, Nazeeruddin and coworkers directly target the between the charge transport layers (CTLs) and $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ in the working mechanism of the $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ solar cells with three different structure (Figure  3c).[41] Their work suggests that the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ is dominated by the splitting of quasi-Fermi levels and recombination in the perovskite bulk (Figure  3d), rather than being governed by any built-in electric field created by the CTL work function difference, which indicates that the electric field is not the dominant charge extraction mechanism for the photo carriers in PSCs. They also identified the nature of the contacts as a critical factor in defining the charge accumulation at its interface, giving rise to either ionic, electronic, or mixed ionic-electronic accumulation.
The role of ETL in PSCs is also illustrated by its effect on the device stability and the $J{-}V$ hysteresis as well as the regularity of device performance parameters. In spite of the stability issues mentioned above for $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ based device, charge accumulation due to mismatched energy level alignment at the $\operatorname{TCO}/$ perovskite interface in ETL-free PSCs may also lead to unstable ETL-free device. In ETL-based device, a cascade energy level alignment can be formed at the TCO/ETL/perovskite interface, which is favor for electron extraction. The large electron barrier between TCO and perovskite (for indium doped tin oxide $(\mathrm{ITO})/$ perovskite interface, this barrier can be as large as about $0.7\:\mathrm{eV}$ could result in severe charge accumulation. As for the effect of charge selective contacts on the device performance and anomalous hysteresis effect in PSCs, Snaith et  al. demonstrated that it is possible to fabricate planar and mesoporous PSCs without an electron selective hole-blocking compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer, which exhibits PCEs of over $13\%.^{[60]}$ This performance is, however, not stable and is related to the anomalous hysteresis in PSCs. The “compact layer-free” meso-superstructured PSCs yield a stabilized PCE of only $2.7\%$ while the ETL-free planar devices display no steady state power output when devoid of an electron selective contact (Figure S3, Supporting Information). This suggests that the hysteresis in ETL-free PSCs can be an important barrier to enhance the performance of such kind of device. While for ETL based device reported in many work, the author could eliminate the hysteresis to achieve record efficiencies for the planar-type and flexible solar cells by optimizing the ETL. In addition, favorable reproducibility of high efficiency can be more easily obtained for devices with ETL relative to that of ETLfree one due to the hole blocking function of the ETL with deep VB level. By optimizing ETL, devices with almost no hysteresis, high robustness and regularity, and high repeatability have been widely reported.[55c–i] While, for ETL-free PSCs the carrier recombination at the TCO/perovskite interface must be carefully addressed. However, considering the brittleness of inorganic materials and complex structure design, ETL-free PSCs exhibit more advantages in cost-effective flexible applications.
In a recent work by Odabaşı and Yildirım, performance analysis of 1921 PSCs was conducted by using machine-learning tools, the performance data points were derived from 800 publications on the PSCs published between 2013 and 2018.[72] The average performance of the device based on the widely used ETL material and efficiencies distribution are shown in Figure S4a,b (Supporting Information), respectively. Obviously, from Figure S4 (Supporting Information), although $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is still the popular option, $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ seems to give higher performances; the cells with $\mathrm{{znO}}$ and without any ETL also have performances comparable to $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . This big data analysis shows that ETL-free PSCs get more attention with their increasing device performance.
## 3.2. The Definition of ETL-Free PSCs
Although the exact definition of the electron transport layer has been roughly given above and thus the definition of ETL-free
PSCs seems self-evident accordingly, that is PSCs without an ETL such as n-type metal oxide and organic semiconductor. In fact, there is, however, another case that needs extra attention. In view of the bipolar carrier transport characteristics of perovskite materials, a part of the perovskite layer itself can actually also be seen as absorb layer and ETL simultaneously. For example, there may arise such a problem: Is perovskite an ETL in devices with the structure TCO/n-type $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ /spiroMeOTAD/Au? This seems to make the definition of ETL-free PSCs controversial. In fact, HTL-free PSCs also encounter the same problem. However, in many published research papers and reviews, the concept of HTL-free PSCs has always been used until now. Therefore, it is necessary to point out here that the ETL-free PSCs are relative to the traditional p–i–n or n–i–p devices, in which the elimination of n-layer in either $\mathrm{{\p-i-n}}$ or n–i–p devices leads to the ETL-free PSCs with p–i or i-p structure. Since the elimination of ETL in general meaning and the fact that perovskite acts mainly as absorb layer in the device TCO/n-type MAPbI3/spiro-MeOTAD/Au, such a device will not be considered as an ETL-free PSC but a Schottky solar cell with TCO electron collection electrode. However, this does not deny that the n-type wide bandgap perovskite in the device $\mathrm{TCO/n}$ - type wide bandgap perovskite/ $\mathbf{\mathrm{MAPbI}}_{3}$ /spiro-MeOTAD/Au is an ETL. Accordingly, either metal/perovskite (MS junction) or metal/insulated layer/perovskite (MIS junction) based devices are ETL-free PSCs. Obviously, ETL-free PSCs include the compact layer-free PSCs, as the compact layer usually refers to a dense $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ (sometime $z_{\mathrm{nO}}$ or any other metal oxides based n-type semiconductor) ETL, and n-type organic semiconductor (fullerene/nonfullerene and their derivatives) ETLs are generally not considered as compact layers.
## 4. ETL-Free PSCs in Varying Structures
## 4.1. Regular Planar Structure
Although most reports in the early literature focused on improving the coverage and density of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL to improve the hole blocking function thus the devices efficiency, where ETL-free devices were commonly set as a control device (Table  1), the first ETL-free PSCs that was formally proposed as the real object of study was demonstrated by Liu et al.[73] The device presented a PCE of $13.5\%$ , very close to that of a $z\mathrm{nO}$ ETL based device, conclusively demonstrating that the ETL is not essentially necessary to obtain excellent device efficiencies. Impedance spectroscopy suggests that while eliminating the $z\mathrm{nO}$ results in an increase in contact resistance, this is offset by a substantial decreased surface recombination.
After their pioneering work, more and more works on ETLfree PSCs were reported. Table 2 summarizes the device performance parameters of ETL-free PSCs reported so far.
## 4.2. Inverted Planar Structure
Most reported ETL-free PSCs adopted an n–i–p configuration in normal structure, while Shi et al. reported the first invert-structured ETL-free PSCs with a multilayer Ti/Au cathode (Figure 4a)
which gives a high PCE close to $13\%$ $(12.91\%$ , Figure  4b) and high reproducibility and hysteresis-free $J{-}V$ curves.[35] The device has a planar structure of (ITO/PEDOT:PSS/perovskite $/\mathrm{Ti}/\mathrm{Au}$ Figure 4c), wherein the Ti interlayer isolates the perovskite and Au layers, hence ensuring good contact between the Au and perovskite and increasing the cells’ shunt resistance greatly. As the Ti/Au cathode in direct contact with perovskite showed no reaction for a long-term in the air, and can provide sufficient protection and avoid the perovskite and PEDOT:PSS layers contact with moisture, the device only lost $\approx30\%$ of their initial efficiency after $300\mathrm{~h~}$ of ambient environment storage. The performance of such a device, however, is mainly limited by its low $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ (0.89 V, smaller than $1\mathrm{~V~}$ as similar with all other PEDOT:PSS based devices), possibly due to the unmatched energy level between perovskite and PEDOT:PSS as founded in previous works.
For PSCs with polycrystalline perovskite films as light absorbing layer, large-aspect-ratio grains are essential for decreased charge recombination at grain boundaries. Previous work proposed that nonwetting HTLs, such as PTAA, can increase nucleus spacing by suppressing heterogeneous nucleation and facilitate grain boundary migration during grain growth by imposing less drag force, thus perovskite films with high average aspect ratio grains and dramatically reduced defect density by 10–100-fold can be achieved.[115] In turn, the reduced grain boundary density and enhanced crystallinity greatly reduce the charge recombination in perovskite bulk to the level of single crystals. Xiao and co-workers reported invert-structured ETL-free PSCs with Ti/Au as composite metal electrode in which nonwetting PTAA as HTL is used to improve perovskite grain size (Figure 4d).[109] With insertion of ultrathin $[10\mathrm{nm}]$ Ti interlayer, the champion device gives a highest PCE of $18.1\%$ (Figure  4e) and preserves ${\approx}90\%$ of the initial PCE after $360\mathrm{~h~}$ (Figure  4f) when stored in $\mathrm{N}_{2}$ filled glove box, which is much stable than the ETL-free device based on PEDOT:PSS HTL as reported by Shi et  al.[35] This represents a great improvement of device stability with metal cathode directly in contact with perovskite film, implying PTAA as HTLs are much more stable than the acidic PEDOT:PSS. As for the enhanced performance, the ultrathin layer of Ti forms a bonding layer with nitrogen (N) atoms in metylammonium anions at perovskite/Ti interface, which passivates surface defects and suppresses surface decomposition of perovskite film as confirmed by X-ray photo­ electron spectroscopy (XPS) and soft X-ray absorption near edge structure measurements. The enhanced stability can be originated from the TiN bonds and the high uniformity and low diffusivity of Ti atoms which acts as a compact blocking layer that prevents the metal atoms from diffusing into the perovskite layer. Also, with Ti interlayer, the device PCE was very close for Au and Cu electrodes, though the work function of Au $(-5.1\ \mathrm{eV})$ and $\mathrm{Cu}\ (-4.3\ \mathrm{eV})$ are very different. This makes the metal electrode more selective and much cheaper Cu can be adopted as electrode to reduce device cost.
## 4.3. Mesoscopic Structure and Schottky Type
Generally speaking, in the earlier studies, a dense $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ compact layer is highly needed for mesoscopic PSCs as perovskite can directly contact with FTO trough mesoscopic channel of nanoporous $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ film, resulting in severe charge recombination as close to the ETL-free device. While, mesoscopic $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ may not only transport electron but also support the perovskite film. Recently, Han and co-workers reported a fully printable mesoscopic PSCs without compact layer and hole conductor that presents a high PCE exceeding $13\%$ (Figure 5a), which is close to that of the device with a $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ compact layer.[92] Instead of the expected direct contact of FTO and perovskite without compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , the author found that the different wettability of the perovskite precursor solution on the surface of different substrates has a significant effect on realizing efficient mesoscopic PSC. Specifically, contact-angle measurements suggest that the perovskite precursor solution showed a significantly different wettability on the FTO substrate and mesoscopic $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\left(\mathrm{m-TiO}_{2}\right)$ film but a similar wettability on the compact $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ $\ensuremath{\left(\mathrm{c}\mathrm{-}\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\right)}$ and $\mathrm{m{\cdot}T i O}_{2}$ (Figure 5b). Due to the capillary effect, the perovskite precursor solution will rise along the pin holes in the $\mathrm{m{\cdot}T i O}_{2}$ layer as the wettability of the perovskite precursor solution on the mp- $\cdot\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is much better than FTO substrate, which may lead to “air cavities” with high resistance and poor contact at the FTO/perovskite interface. Therefore, for such mesoscopic structured “ETL-free” PSCs, mesoscopic $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ is the actual ETL. Despite of the nominal “ETL-free” of such a PSC, this result shows a promising future in printable solar cells with simplified fabrication process and much lowered preparation costs. Song et al. study PSCs based on mesoporous $\mathrm{{Al}}_{2}\mathrm{{O}}_{3}$ scaffold infiltrated and capped with a perovskite absorber layer.[88] They employ dipolar organic compound ethoxylated PEIE to decorate the FTO/perovskite interface, which induced a strong dipole moment at the interface. As a result a $9.1\mathrm{nm}$ of PEIE endows the device a PCE up to $15\%$ .
Zhuo et  al. reported the first Schottky solar cells without ETL and HTL based on $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{SnI}_{3}$ , the cell consists of a much simpler structure of $\mathrm{ITO/CsSnI_{3}/A u/T i}$ (Figure  5d).[74] The
ITO electrode sputtered has a thickness of about $80\ \mathrm{nm}$ and a sheet resistance of about 50 ohm/square, which limits the series resistance. The polycrystalline quality of the film is poor, leading to a small shunt resistance. Thus the PCE is only $0.9\%$ resulting from a low filling factor of $22\%$ . The effect of light intensity on $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ and $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ supports the Schottky solar cell model. The work-function difference of $0.6\ \mathrm{eV}$ between the Au and ITO is expected to build up the built-in potential energy barrier near the interface, as the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ is consistent with the workfunction difference of $0.6\mathrm{~V~}$ between the anode and cathode.
Also, the built-in electric field at Schottky junction was confirmed by the spectrally resolved $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ measurement. With the unintentional p-type doping characteristics of the $\mathrm{CsSnI}_{3}$ due to the easy oxidation of $\mathrm{Sn}^{2+}$ to $\mathrm{Sn^{4+}}$ as many previous works proposed, $\mathrm{CsSnI_{3}/I T O}$ contact can be a quasi ohmic contact, so it is very important to control the doping type and level of the $\mathrm{CsSnI}_{3}$ perovskite for further enhance the device performance. Wu et  al. reported glass/FTO/perovskite/Au structured ETLand HTL-free PSC with fullerene incorporated into perovskite layer and the fullerene within perovskite layer forms a localized dipole-like electric field that promotes charge separation, resulting in a PCE of $3.53\%$ . While the pristine perovskite without the assistance of ETL and HTL shows no photovoltaic performance.
To enhance light absorption and photoelectric properties of Schottky PSCs, Ming et  al. adopted $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}–\mathrm{PbS}$ core–shell NPs to assemble PSC with a structure of FTO/MAPbI –PbS core–shell NPs/Al, which presented a much higher PCE of $2.07\%$ than $0.9\%$ reported by Chen et  al.[95] With a small bandgap of 0.67 eV, PbS QD enhanced the infrared light absorption as inferred from incident photon-to-electron conversion efficiency (IPCE) spectra. More recently, Tang and co-workers reported a further simplified $\mathrm{FTO}/\mathrm{CsPbBr}_{3}$ carbon device (Figure  5e) with much simplified processes and reduced production costs.[107] A large energy barrier between $E_{\mathrm{{F}}}$ of FTO and $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ as well as valence band of $\mathrm{Cs}\mathrm{Pb}{\mathrm{Br}}_{3}$ and $E_{\mathrm{F}}$ of carbon electrode markedly increases the recombination and a PCE of $2.35\%$ is achieved. Attributing to the intrinsic stable properties of inorganic perovskite, the device without encapsulation can maintain $80\%$ of their initial values over $200\mathrm{{h}}$ when preserved at $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ and humidity of $50\%$ RH. Their works demonstrates the potential of all inorganic perovskite as stable light absorber in robust ETL-free PSCs.
By far most PSCs adopted a vertical configurations with nonsymmetrical electrode with different work functions as carrier selective contact, the device with symmetrical electrode work functions usually showed no photovoltaic performance with zero $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ due to the symmetrical electrodes without directional carrier select. Huang and co-workers reported the first PSC with symmetrical electrode work functions as shown in Figure $5\mathrm{f.}^{[76]}$ The fresh devices have a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $8.5\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}$ and $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $0.18\mathrm{V},$ in spite of the same work functions of PEDOT:PSS and Au electrodes. Also, the photocurrent direction can be switched repeatedly by applying a electric field of $<1\mathrm{\V\upmum^{-1}}$ , such switchable diode in the dark can result in a giant switchable photovoltaic effect under illumination (Figure  5g). Many computation and experiments have shown that positive ions/vacancies can n-type dope while negative space charges can p-type dope the perovskite materials, thus an asymmetric distribution of space charges in perovskite layer may result in a $\mathtt{p-i-n}$ or $\mathrm{{\underline{{n-i-p}}}}$ homojunction structure that can potentially enhance the apparent performance of PSCs. This switchable photovoltaic effect can be rationalized by the formation of reversible p–i–n homojunction structure induced by ion drift and electric-field-manipulated p- and n-type doping of the perovskite layer by external electric field as confirmed by KPFM. Based on such an external electric field-induced selfpoling effect, soon after, light-induced self-poling effect of PSCs (Figure 5h) was also demonstrated to promote the formation of in situ p–i–n homojunction structure (Figure 5i) as the light-generated photovoltage also causes directional drift of ions/vacancies in the same way as the applied external bias.[116–119] These spontaneous p–i–n homojunction formation by field and illumination induced electrochemical doping could relieve the strict structure requirement and simplify the fabrication procedure for PSCs by paving the way for innovative solar cell designs.
Table  3 summarizes the photovoltaic parameters of mesoscopic structure and Schottky structure reported so far.
## 4.4. MIS Structure
Zheng et  al. reported ETL-free devices with a ultrathin watersoluble polythiophene PTEBS (sodium poly[2-(3-thienyl)ethyloxy-4-butylsulfonate]) as a cathode interlayer (Figure  6a).[99] Favorable energy level alignment and improved film morphology can be obtained with an ultrathin PTEBS layer. Ohmic contact at the perovskite/cathode interface also benefits the charge transport and extraction. Moreover, inhibited charge accumulation at the interface weakens the polarization of the perovskite and results in a relatively quick response of the modified device. The device achieved PCE of $15.4\%$ without obvious hysteresis effect (Figure  6b). While the device without this interlayer presents a low PCE of $7.81\%$ and severe hysteresis (Figure 6c). With its large bandgap of $2{\mathrm{eV}}$ and low LUMO level between $-2.7$ and $-3.1\ \mathrm{eV}_{:}$ , there exists a large valance band offset of $0.8\mathrm{-}1.2\ \mathrm{eV},$ thus PTEBS does not favor for electron extraction from energetic point of view. Therefore, the ultrathin PTEBS should not be regarded as a ETL but rather as an insulated tunneling layer. The MS junction of ITO/perovskite interface is changed to be a MIS (metal–insulator–semiconductor)
junction with this interlayer. Zhang et  al. adopted ALD to deposit thickness controllable ${\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ layer as insulated layer to construct MIS junction for mesoporous structured ETL-free device (Figure  6d).[121] An highest efficiency of $16.2\%$ for the sample with 50 ALD cycles $(\approx5\ \mathrm{nm})$ , which is a significant improvement over interlayer-free PSCs, which have a maximum efficiency of $11.0\%$ (Figure 6e). The ultrathin ${\mathrm{Al}}_{2}{\mathrm{O}}_{3}$ layer can effectively prevent electron recombination at the perovskite/FTO interface and can efficiently transport the electrons by tunneling (Figure 6f).
Apart from these ETL-free PSC based on MIS junction, Zhao et al. employed a $40\ \mathrm{nm}$ thick “insulated” wide bandgap interlayer BCP to fabricate nominal MIS junction based ETL-free devices (Figure 6g).[106] PCE of the devices were improved from $15.56\%$ to $19.07\%$ after inserting the BCP layer (Figure  6h). Moreover, the devices exhibit outstanding photostability: the unsealed PSCs lost $\approx10\%$ of their initial PCS after $1000\mathrm{~h~}$ of storage in air and maintain $92.2\%$ after $450\mathrm{~h~}$ of exposure to full solar irradiation (without a UV filter), compared to only $14.1\%$ in the $\mathrm{{\n-i-p}}$ devices with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ as ETL under the same condition. In spite of the outstanding performance, the role of the thick BCP interlayer is not very clear, as the insulator is too thick for electron to tunnel through it. We note that the electron transport mechanism of BCP as exciton blocking layer in many organic optoelectronic devices has been gradually recognized and revealed. Lee et  al. found that chemical interaction between deposited Al and BCP can induce electron states in the BCP bandgap, through which electrons can transport.[122] The Al/BCP interaction induces both transport and trap levels in the energy gap of BCP and their interplay results in the electron transport property observed. Sakurai et al. studied the effect of gap states on electrical properties at interface between bathocuproine and several metals via photoelectron spectroscopy.[123] Morikawa and co-workers conducted theoretical study on electronic structure of BCP and found that the nature of the unoccupied electronic states is a key characteristic that plays vital roles in the electron transport.[124] Based on these pioneering work, we think that the BCP-based device is not veritable ETL-free PSC with MIS junction but conventional $\mathrm{{\underline{{n-i-p}}}}$ device with BCP as ETL, in which electron transport trough the bandgap state (Figure 6i).[125]
## 4.5. Lateral Structure
Apart from the common vertically stacked multilayer structured device where perovskite layer are usually sandwiched between ETL and HTL, lateral structured device was only reported very recently. Khoram et  al. reported a microscopic PSC based on single microcrystals perovskite grown on prefabricated grating electrodes with nonsymmetric work functions (Figure 7a,b) creating single-crystal devices contacted from the back (bc-PSC, Figure 7c–f).[126] Such a microscale PSC with high (Pd) and low (Ti) work function metals as carrier-selective contacts gives a high $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.07\mathrm{~V~}$ (Figure $\mathrm{7g)}$ ). With symmetric back-contact, such a device shows no obvious photovoltaic characteristics, demonstrating the importance of the nonsymmetric Schottky junction. Bach et al. reported a simple CTL-free PSC that comprises only a perovskite layer with two interdigitated gold backcontacts (Figure 7h), in which charge extraction is achieved via small molecular SAMs with opposite polarity and their inherent dipole fields at the metal/perovskite interface (Figure 7i).[36] The device gives a $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $0.55\mathrm{V}$ (Figure 7j) and a highest performing bc-PSC showed a PCE of $3.11\%$ . For such double Schottky junction solar cells, the built-in potential originated from the different work function of the two contacts is crucial for charge extraction. The work function difference of the two contacts can be realized either by adopting different metals[126] or by surface polarity modification of metal electrode.[36]
Inserting interdigitated back-contact electrodes into PSCs overcomes the optical losses by eliminating the need for transparent electrodes and low architectural defect tolerance present in conventional “sandwich” cell configurations. However, the small charge-carrier diffusion length within the polycrystalline perovskite film relative to the grating electrode spacing can limit the device performance. By changing the perovskite films grain sizes in the bc-PSC, Bach and co-workers further found that the charge-carrier diffusion length is crystal-size dependent and the device performance correlates positively with the perovskite grain size.[127] Such a nonsymmetric backcontacted device platform is also helpful for understanding the effect of film morphology on the optoelectrical property and the final device performance. For example, to reveal the effects of $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ on the performance of the final devices, Cho and co-workers fabricated lateral device (Figure  7k) with perovskite with and without $\mathrm{Cl^{-}}$ to observe charge carrier diffusion and found that the charge carrier pathways in the mixed-halide perovskite with a longer charge carrier diffusion length are more efficient.[110] The lateral $\mathrm{Al/MAPbI_{3-x}C l_{\it x}/M o O_{3}/A u}$ device gives a highest PCE of $2.4\%$ (Figure 7l). The $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ is strongly influenced by variations in the electrode gap (Figure $7\mathrm{m}$ ) as charge carrier would encounter more GBs when a wide gap is adopted. Different from the vertical structure device, where the grain can be seen single crystal on the charge transport path in the vertical direction, and the charge is mainly transmitted along the inside of the grain, in the current lateral structured device charge carriers have to pass through multiple grain boundaries (especially when the grain size is much smaller than the electrode gap) and can be easily recombined induced by scattering and defects associated with grain boundaries. With tunable electrodes gap, this device design makes it possible to directly examine the entire channel area with varying microscopic and spectroscopic techniques. Some photo detectors also adopt this structure.[128,129]
Similar with the vertical-structured PSCs with symmetrical electrode work functions, there are also report on lateralstructured PSCs with symmetrical electrode work functions. Huang and co-workers reported PSCs with symmetrical $\mathrm{Au}/$ $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}/\mathrm{Au}$ configuration (Figure  8a).[76] The ionic electromigration induced reversible $\mathtt{p-i-n}$ and n–i–p homojunction as suggested by KPFM measurement has been recognized as the origin of the switchable photovoltaic effect in such devices as in the symmetrical vertical-structured PSCs. The poled single cell showed a $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $0.48{\mathrm{~V~}}$ under 0.25 sun illumination after poling at $10\mathrm{~V~}$ for about 100 s (Figure  8b). Moreover, such lateral structure devices can be connected in series, outputting a photovoltage which is the sum of the photovoltage of each device. For example, a large $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $70\mathrm{V}$ was observed for devices with 175 unit cells connected in series under illumination of 0.25 sun. Nevertheless, the carrier diffusion lengths in the polycrystalline PSCs are too small due to the strong scattering of charge carriers at the grain boundaries, which results in the low $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and FF, thus the highest PCE obtained with polycrystalline lateral-structure PSCs was below $0.1\%$ . Further replacing the polycrystalline perovskite films with the single crystals (Figure  8c,d) yields an efficiency enhancement of 44-folds (Figure  8e), enabled by much larger carrier diffusion lengths as demonstrated by a 33 times larger $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ than that of the polycryalline devices.[117] More recently, they reported the observation of an anomalous photovoltaic (APV) effect at room temperature in lateral devices manifested by the device’s $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ that is much larger than the bandgap of perovskite (Figure  8f,g).[118] The persistent $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ is proportional to the electrode space, resembling that of ferroelectric photovoltaic devices. However, the APV effect here is not caused by ferroelectricity but by the formation of tunneling junctions (Figure  8i) randomly distributed in the polycrystalline films induced by ion accumulation (Figure  8h), which leads to the accumulation of photovoltage at a macroscopic level. Figure 8j presents illustration of the proposed mechanism for APV effect induced by dispersed tunneling junction.
Table  4 summarizes the photovoltaic parameters of lateral structure ETL-free PSCs reported so far.
## 4.6. Flexible Cells, Semitransparent Cells
Shin and co-workers reported a flexible ETL-free device (Figure  9a) that accomplished a high PCE of ${\approx}8.39\%$ along with a high $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $\approx15.24\ensuremath{\mathrm{\mA\cm^{-2}}}$ , a $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of ${\approx}0.830\ \mathrm{V},$ and a high FF of ${\approx}0.66$ . The configuration is $\mathrm{PET/ITO/Ti}/$ $\mathrm{CH_{3}N H_{3}P b I_{3}/s p i r o-M e O T A D/A g.^{[77]}}$ The effects of titanium (Ti) layer thickness on the charge transport and recombination rates of flexible PSCs were studied. In spite of the lower device efficiency resulted from a low transmittance of the flexible substrate (Figure 9b), the flexibility and peculiar configuration of the device exhibit a certain application prospects. Yang et al. reported an efficient and flexible PSC based on $\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ with simplified ETL-free configuration which achieved a champion efficiency of $12.70\%$ (Figure 9c,d).[82] The underlying working mechanism is proposed via a systematic investigation of the heterojunction property of this device and it was observed that a significant charge storage in the perovskite is believed to generate photovoltage and serves as the driving force for charge transferring from the absorber to ITO electrode. After bending 20 times, the $J{-}V$ curve demonstrating a decreased FF in comparison with the pristine one (Figure  9d), which is due to the increased resistance in Au electrode with small cracks after bending. Therefore, the mechanical properties of the metal electrode is also important for the bendability of flexible device. Lee and co-workers reported the use of PEI modified PEDOT:PSS as the cathode to fabricate flexible ITO and ETL-free device on the polyethylene terephthalate (PET) substrates that exhibits a PCE of $9.73\%$ .[96] The modification by PEI significantly changed the work function of PEDOT:PSS from $-5.06$ to $-4.08\ \mathrm{eV};$ ensuring better aligned energy levels with perovskite and electron extraction. The device also shows good bendability, with a bending radius about $3\mathrm{mm}$ and after 500 bends, the device’s PCE was $6.75\%$ , corresponding to the degradation of up to $31\%$ (Figure  9e). Wei et  al. used DMFGBL (1,4-butyrolactone) as mixed solvent and toluene as an antisolvent to construct dense, mirror-like, pinhole-free, and semitransparent perovskite film (Figure 9f).[106] With superior electron extraction ability of the enhanced perovskite film, ETL-free PSC device with a PCE of $12.6\%$ is attained. Further simplified the device fabrication process by removing the annealing step resulted in ETL-free PSCs with a highest PCE of $10.5\%$ . The remove of thermal annealing can greatly simplify the fabrication procedure of PSC, promising its potential applications in flexible device.
## 5. Working Principle and Factors Influencing Performance
## 5.1. Working Principle
The underlying working mechanism of ETL-free device is proposed subsequently by some groups. Ke et al. proposed that the operation mechanism for their ETL-free device may be similar to that of a $\mathtt{p-i-n}$ device, as the FTO substrates adopted have a sheet resistance of 14 Ohms per square and can be seen as a $\mathrm{n}^{++}$ electrodes.[78] Meanwhile, the deposited perovskite films are highly resistive and have low carrier concentrations, typically below the sensitivity of the Hall measurement system $(\approx10^{14}\mathrm{cm}^{-3})$ . As a degenerate semiconductor, highly doped FTO can form a metal–semiconductor (MS) contact with perovskite, such a MS junction with a Schottky barrier have good rectifying characteristic. By KPFM measurement, Yang et al. revealed significant charge storage in the perovskite layer, which is believed to generate photovoltage and serves as the driving force for charge transfer from the absorber to ITO electrode.[82] As the work function of ITO $({\approx}4.7~\mathrm{eV})$ is higher than the Fermi level of $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ , Han et  al. suggest that the band alignment at the $\mathrm{ITO/MAPbI}_{3}$ interface ensures a band bending direction of $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ downward and the built-in electric field at the interface points from ITO to $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ which attracts electrons but repels holes (Figure 10a).[111] In addition, ITO is a typical n-type semiconductor whose valence band maximum (VBM) edge is lower than perovskite, which makes the as-formed $\mathrm{ITO/MAPbI}_{3}$ interface preferable for electron extraction and hole blocking. Based on this analysis, the performance loss in ETL-free PSC may not be due to the lack of hole blocking layer. While such claim is just a guess based on device performance and a previous report.
In fact, the interface energy level alignment at the ITO (FTO)/ $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ interface is highly dependent on the relative position of the Fermi levels of ITO (FTO) and $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ .[130–132] For given ITO (FTO) with a constant work function, this depends on the doping type and concentration of the perovskite film. Specific interface band structure requires photoelectron characterization. Using ultraviolet photoelectron spectroscopy (UPS), Olthof and Meerholz investigated the energy level alignment of $\mathrm{ITO}/$ perovskite interface by incrementally depositing $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ by coevaporation method on ITO substrate (Figure  10b).[131] The observed downward band bending in the perovskite (Figure 10c) suggests the n-type nature of the prepared $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ and the work function of perovskite is smaller than ITO. The reported $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of ETL-free PSCs are $0.8\mathrm{-}1.0\mathrm{V},$ close to that of the HTL-free PSCs. The observed upward band bending toward interface can hinder electron transport and thereby deteriorate the device performance. However, if the work function of perovskite is higher than ITO, the interface band bending could be inverted to the case like Han et  al. suggests (Figure  10a).[111] In this case the perovskite should be weaker n- or p-type. To control the relative position of the Fermi levels of ITO (FTO) and $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ , one needs to tail the polarity of the perovskite film. In their recent work by Huang et  al., by fitting analysis the heterojunction characteristics of the ETL-free devices, it is found that, when fabricated with perovskite of unoptimized polarity, such devices are not ideal single heterojunction devices but a Schottky/p–n cascade heterojuction devices consists of FTO/perovskite MS junction and perovskite/spiro-MeOTAD p–n heterojunction in combination, as suggests by the ideality factor exceeding 2.[54] Thus, to improve the performance of such device, the band bending at both of the two interfaces should be considered as a whole. Realizing this, efficient n-type intentional doping with $\mathrm{Sb}^{3+}$ was conducted to adjust the polarity of the perovskite films and to optimize the interface band bending. With this, the energy band bending at FTO/perovskite interface is favor for electron extraction and hole blocking, while the band bending at perovskite/spiro-MeOTAD interface promotes efficient hole collection and electron blocking.
By conducting UPS measurement for the as-synthesized Cl$\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ , Yang et al. found that the sample has a work function of about $4.6\:\mathrm{eV}$ and can be seen as an n-type semiconductor, therefore a p–n junction may form at the Cl-FAPbI3/spiro-MeOTAD
AZO surface region can sweep the electrons away from the interface, reducing their recombination rate with holes. Thus, $\mathrm{AZO}/$ undoped-AZO/perovskite interface with lower energy losses results in a highest efficiency of $10.5\%$ and $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $1.05\mathrm{V}.$
interface.[82] Further, by KPFM measurement, the relative values of effective work function of ITO and $\mathrm{Cl-FAPbI}_{3}$ can be determined by the contact potential difference (CPD): $V_{\mathrm{CPD}}=(\phi_{\mathrm{tip}}-\phi_{\mathrm{sample}})/e$ . Where $\phi_{\mathrm{sample}}$ and $\phi_{\mathrm{tip}}$ are the work functions of the sample and tip, and $e$ is the element charge. $\phi_{\mathrm{tip}}$ can be seen as a constant value with the same scanning tip during the measurement. As shown in Figure  11a,b, the inserted potential line suggests the CPD of both ITO and Cl$\mathrm{FAPbI}_{3}$ are ${\approx}1.2~\mathrm{V}.$ The difference of energy level in two materials is too small to form a built-in potential. Different from the above view of the energy band structure at the TCO/perovskite interface, however Chouhan and Avasthi suggested that AZO can function as effective ETL and transparent electrode in their ETL-free PSC with AZO/MAPbI3/spiro-MeOTAD/Au structure.[113] Electronic band diagram derived from the UPS measurement shows that AZO/perovskite is a carrier-selective interface that can block holes but allows transport of electrons. Exposing AZO surface to ozone can significantly improve the performance of the AZO/perovskite interface, as XPS measurement demonstrates that ozone scavenges oxygen vacancies in the top $6{\-}9\ \mathrm{nm}$ of the AZO surface, leading to the formation of a highly-doped-AZO/undoped-AZO junction. As AZO is highly doped, energy bands in AZO are flat. The difference in work function, $\mathrm{\approx}0.1\ \mathrm{eV},$ falls mainly across the perovskite film (Figure  11c). In ozone-treated AZO, there exists a thin-region of “undoped” layer near the AZO surface. So, the band bending falls across not only perovskite but also undoped AZO surface layer (Figure  11d). The upward band bending in the undoped
Apart from the interface electronic structure and band bending, the dynamics and energetics of the interface charge transfer are also very important for efficient PSCs. To probe the electron transfer and carrier separation processes at the FTO/perovskite interface, steady-state PL spectroscopy was carried out. It is found that a substantial quenching effect can take place when the perovskite is direct in contact with FTO. Similar to the PL quenching degree of the perovskite film deposited on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ , $\approx70\%$ PL quenching can be observed when the perovskite was deposited on FTO, demonstrating the FTO as an efficient electron extraction cathode to ensure high efficient ETL-free device.[55] A similar phenomena was also observed by Liu et  al. for the $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ film deposited on $z_{\mathrm{{nO}}}$ and ITO.[73] To investigate the carrier extraction dynamics, time-resolved photoluminescence (TRPL) decays are conducted by Peng et  al.[94] It is revealed that the doping type of perovskite greatly affects the TRPL decay profiles. Generally, an n-type perovskite ensures much fast interface charge . When depositing spiro-MeOTAD on the perovskite surface, hole extraction by HTL occurs at the perovskite/HTL interface, resulting in the promoted decay in PL intensity. The ows a better hole extraction from the n-type perovskite film than from the p-type perovskite film, suggesting the forof the perovskite/HTL p–n heterojunction that can enhance hole extraction at perovskite/HTL interface. Their is in well consistent with the results reported by Huang et al.[54] The kinetics of the luminescence of perovskite films in osecond time range has also been studied. In the case of $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ on the surface of FTO, the luminescence kinetics has an exponential form of damping with a lifetime of 22.3 ns, much shorter than the life time of electron, suggesting effiectron extraction before recombination in the span of carrier life time.[133]
## 5.2. Factors Affecting Device Performance
## 5.2.1. Work Function of TCO and Back Electrode
Liu and co-workers studied the effect of different kind of transparent conductive oxide (TCO) on the performance of ETL-free PSCs (Figure  12a).[89] Their results showed that, though the little morphology of the perovskite films deposited on different TCOs (ITO, FTO, and AZO) leads to small difference on charge transfer and recombination, all devices show almost similar performance (about $10\%$ ) (Figure  12b).
The difference of devices should be derived from the small difference in work functions of each TCO. It is reported in the work by Yu et  al., by modifying the surface of the commercially purchased FTO to a porous structure using electrochemical etching, the charge extraction at the FTO/perovskite interface can be accelerated (Figure  12c).[102] This surface engineering leads to a significant increase in the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ and eliminates the hysteretic behavior of the device based on the pristine FTO. As a result, the average PCE was enhanced from 16.80 to $17.61\%$ . The PCE improvement originated from a drastic growth in the $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ that was attributed to the expedited expulsion of unbound electrons in the perovskite due to an increased contact area between the perovskite and the E-FTO (Figure  12c,d). The light trapping and good wettability can also contribute to the enhancement of the performance, but to a much lesser extent. Also, the slightly lower $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ was due to a small increase of the work function with surface modification (Figure 12e). The highest PCE of $19.22\%$ was once a recorded value for an ETL-free PSC (Figure  12f), slightly higher than the simulated value $(19.16\%)$ reported by Huang et al. in their previous simulation.
For ETL-free $\mathrm{PSCs^{[91]}}$ with normal structure, their performance depends highly on the work function of the TCO adopted. Hu et al. reported ETL-free PSC with $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ modified ITO cathode as efficient electron selective contact (Figure 13a), a remarkable PCE of $15.1\%$ was achieved.[75] The surface modification of ITO by $\mathrm{Cs}_{2}\mathrm{CO}_{3}$ lowers the work function of ITO by $\mathtt{C s-O}$ bonds generated on the ITO surface, contributing to an optimized energy level alignment and improved ohmic contact at the ITO/perovskite interface (Figure  13b). Song et  al. study PSCs with mesoporous alumina scaffold and a perovskite cap layer, which are devoid of an ETL.[88] They employ PEIE to modify the FTO/perovskite interface. The PEIE interlayer induced a strong dipole moment at the FTO/perovskite interface due to electrons accumulation in the perovskite film near the interface. The resultant “n-type” region near the perovskite surface, progressing to an intrinsic (i) region within the perovskite bulk, forms an n–i homojunction that favorably enables selective extraction of electron at the FTO electrode. As a result a $9.1\ \mathrm{nm}$ of PEIE endows the device a highest PCE of $15\%$ . Chen et  al. employed PEI modified PEDOT:PSS as the cathode to fabricate flexible ETL-free PSC on PET substrates (Figure 13c).[96] The modification by PEI significantly decreases the work function of PEDOT:PSS from 5.06 to $4.08\mathrm{eV},$ yielding better interface energy level alignment and electron extraction (Figure 13d). The resulted flexible device exhibited a PCE of $9.73\%$ and good bendability (Figure  13e). Li et  al. adopted chlorinated FTO (Cl-FTO) electrode (Figure  13f) with a high work function (Figure  13g) to fabricate ETL-free PSC.[100] The increased work function of Cl-FTO originated from increased surface vacuum energy level allows well-matched energy levels between FTO and the perovskite layer, accelerating the electron extraction and collection (Figure  13h). Accordingly, a highest PCE of $13.39\%$ was demonstrated. Zhan et  al. reported ETLfree PSC with anodized ITO electrode.[105] Compared to the devices fabricated from untreated ITO substrates, this simple surface anodization treatment can improve the PCE from $8.68\%$ to $11.78\%$ due to reduced recombination.
In contrast to the normal structure, the device performances of the invert-structured ETL-free PSCs with Ti interlayer are not sensitive to the work function of the back electrode. With Ti interlayer, the device PCE was very close to that of Au and Cu electrodes based devices, in spite of the significant difference in work function of $\mathrm{Cu}\ (-4.3\mathrm{eV})$ and Au $(-5.1\mathrm{eV})$ . This makes the metal electrode more selective.[109]
## 5.2.2. Coverage of Perovskite Film
For planar n–i–p device, even with no $100\%$ perovskite film coverage, the device can still present a high efficiency exceeding $10\%$ , and it seems that the pinholes in perovskite films do not severely affect the device performance.[86] The $I{-}V$ respond of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2},$ /spiro-MeOTAD heterojunction based device (Figure  14a) suggests that such a heterojunction has well rectifying characteristics which can avoid the device from being short circuited. While for ETL-free device, the case is much different. Huang et al. demonstrated systematically the crucial role of high perovskite coverage in high-efficiency ETL-free PSCs from an energy band and equivalent circuit point of view. Figure  14b,c gives the dependence of the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of the ETL-free device on perovskite coverage derived from numerical simulation, which implies a severe influence of perovskite coverage on the performance of the ETL-free device.[41,86] Further experiment suggests that when coverage decreases, the device performance drops significantly. Kang and co-workers reported a large grain based ETL-free PSCs with highest efficiency of $18.20\%$ . By using a $58\mathrm{\mt}\%$ stock solution while maintaining the high crystallinity, the grain size of $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ can be as large as ${\ \approx}1\ \upmu\mathrm{m}$ .[93] These $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ films with large grain can hinder HTL permeation effectively, enabling a reduction in carrier recombination. Accordingly, the top performing ETL-free PSC had a
PCE of $18.20\%$ , $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $21.45~\mathrm{mA~cm}^{-2}$ , $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of 1.127 V, and FF of $75.1\%$ . This PCE was once the highest value compared with the same type of PSCs and is most closely matched with a previous simulation research. Huang et al. found that plasma treatment of FTO could significantly improve the perovskite films quality and promote charge separation, leading to an increase in the electron-transport rate, and a decrease in the interface carrier recombination.[101] With the high quality perovskite films deposited on the plasma pretreated FTO substrate, ETL-free PSCs with a highest PCE of $15.42\%$ were demonstrated.
## 5.2.3. Polarity of Perovskite Film
The doping type and concentration of perovskite, as a fundamental parameter of a semiconductor, greatly affect the cell performance. Cui et  al. reported highly efficient ETL-free PSCs (Figure  15a) by forming effective p–n heterojunction though defects engineering enabled self-doping of the perovskite, which can change the doping type from p to n, and the n-type self-doping degree can be tuned serially by precursor composition and process conditions (annealing temperature, Figure  15b,c).[94] Accordingly, ETL-free PSCs with n-type selfdoped perovskite films exhibit a highest efficiency of $15.69\%$ . The KPFM measurement was conducted to confirm the $\mathtt{p-n}$ heterojunction and depletion field at the spiro-MeOTAD/perovskite interface (Figure 15d), as demonstrated by the local CPD distribution between tip and sample across the spiro-MeOTAD and perovskite surface (Figure  15e,f). By device simulation, Li and co-workers investigated the effects of perovskite doping type, doping concentration, and films thickness on the optoelectronic response of the ETL-free PSCs in term of energy band bending, built-in electrical field, and the surface/bulk recombination.[134] They found that a decreased work function of FTO $(4.6\mathrm{-}4.0\ \mathrm{~eV})$ can suppress the recombination at the FTO/perovskite interface and greatly improve the efficiency. With optimized parameters of n-type doping concentration of $1\times10^{17}\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , the PCE can be promoted to be $18.5\%$ when a Schottky contact is formed between FTO and perovskite. By intentional polarity control of perovskite via incorporation of Sb as a shallow donor, Huang et  al. reported ETL-free PSCs with well-matched energy level alignment both at front and rear interfaces. The enhanced built-in electric field thus efficient majority carrier collection and minority blocking as well as reduced interfacial charge recombination at both interfaces (Figure $10\mathrm{e},\mathrm{f})^{[54]}$ leads to ETL-free PSCs with a Schottky/p–n cascade heterojunction that achieves a maximum efficiency of $12.62\%$ , demonstrating new solutions to further enhance the device performance of ETL-free PSCs.
## 5.2.4. Carrier Life Time of Perovskite Film
Hu and co-workers reported ETL-free device with mixed perovskite $\mathrm{FA}_{0.83}\mathrm{Cs}_{0.17}\mathrm{Pb}(\mathrm{I}_{0.6}\mathrm{Br}_{0.4})_{3}$ .[111] The average grain size, the thickness, and absorbance of the perovskite film increase approximately linearly with precursor concentration. Perovskite films with a large grain size exceeding $12\upmu\mathrm{m}$ can be achieved by adopting a $1.25~\mathrm{~M~}$ concentration of perovskite precursor. Based on such film, the device exhibited a best-performing PCE of $13.80\%$ and prolonged stability, which can benefit from the prolonged carrier life time with a larger grain size. Han et  al. found that the performance of ETL-free PSCs is far more sensitive to the carrier lifetime of perovskite films. A low electroninjection rate at the ETL-free interface is responsible for the severe energy loss. A long carrier lifetime can counterbalance the inferior interface with inferior electrons extraction in the absence of an ETL, enabling fast electron collection in ETL-free PSC. It is found that excessive $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ significantly increases the carrier lifetimes and the PL quenching rate of the $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ films, hence the device performance (Figure  16c–e). By using thick perovskite films (about $700\ \mathrm{nm}$ $\mathrm{Cs_{0.05}F A_{0.8}M A_{0.15}P b I_{2.55}B r_{0.45})}$ (Figure  16a,b) with microsecond carrier lifetime (Figure  16e), they achieved ETL-free PSCs with $19.52\%$ PCE (Figure  16f), which is close to the value of ETL based devices. These results indicate that optimization of perovskite film preparation process, component, and thus quality can further improve the device performance of ETL-free PSCs.
## 5.2.5. Effect of Additive
The codeposition of perovskite and electron conductors has been employed to fabricate ETL-free PSCs as well. Pascual et al. deposited an $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}{:}\mathrm{C}_{70}$ blend layer to fabricate ETL-free devices.[90] The incorporation of $\mathrm{C}_{70}$ significantly eliminated pinholes in the film and suppresses charge carrier recombination. As a result, the $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}{:}\mathrm{C}_{70}$ based PSCs exhibited larger recombination resistance than the pristine $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ based ones, showing a highest PCE of $13.6\%$ . Also, the photostability of $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}{:}\mathrm{C}_{70}$ based PSCs without encapsulation was enhanced. Soon after, they confirmed the nature of the cosolvent interacting with fullerene to eliminate pinholes as a determining factor in achieving enhanced devices. They also found that electron transfer happens from $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ to $\mathrm{C}_{70}$ in the blend films, which can be enhanced by being processed using aromatic cosolvent.[135] Delgado and co-workers reported a series of novel modified fullerenes, showing varying electron accepting capabilities, to prepare ETL-free PSCs based on perovskite:fullerene blends.[97] A highest PCE of $14.3\%$ was demonstrated. In addition, a clear correlation between the LUMO level of the fullerene component and the $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of the devices is found. Similarly, ${\mathbb{W}}{\mathrm{{u}}}$ et  al. found that the incorporated fullerene within the perovskite layer (Figure $\mathrm{17g}$ ) can form a dipole-like localized electric field (Figure  17h) that favors charge separation, resulting in significant device performance improvement.[136] To reveal the fullerene induced dipole-assisted screen effect that facilitates the electron transport, the device without ETL and HTL was fabricated to rule out the influence of the CTL. Without HTL and ETL, the higher charge transport properties enabled by incorporated fullerene can be directly visualized. Without the assistance of ETL and HTL, the pristine perovskite shows no photovoltaic response. While the fullerene incorporated PSC shows a $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ of $16.8\mathrm{mAcm}^{-2}$ , a $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ of $0.74\mathrm{V},$ and a PCE of $3.53\%$ (Figure 17i).
As the performances of ETL-free PSCs are mainly limited by severe carrier recombination induced by current leakage (insufficient perovskite film coverage) and inferior charge extraction. In the work by Wang and co-workers, an MACl additive is used to construct an intriguing bilayer perovskite film featuring a compact bottom layer and a maze-like top layer.[114] Compared with the flat films produced from the conventional method (Figure  17a,c), such maze-like perovskite films (Figure  17b,d) enable the ETL-free PSCs (Figure  17e) with a highest PCE of $18.5\%$ with negligible hysteresis, which can be due to the higher crystallinity hence superior light-harvesting capability of the resultant perovskite film, as well as accelerated hole extraction at the perovskite/HTL interface (Figure 17f).
In addition to electronics optimization, from the view point of light absorption enhancement, Sebag et  al. studied the impact of introducing $\mathrm{Yb}^{3+}/\mathrm{Er}^{3+}$ co-doped KY7F22 upconversion nanoparticles into the different interfaces of a ETL-free mixed perovskite based PSC.[108] Due to the near-infrared light harvest induced by optical upconversion, about $5\%$ of averaged $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ enhancement was obtained, contributing to the observed averaged PCE enhancement of $7.4\%$ . Chen et  al. introduced copper–silver alloy nanoparticles in to ETL-free PSCs to enhance the absorption of the perovskite layer by effectively light scattering in a broadband wavelength range and achieves a series resistance decrease of the device due to the high conductibility of the alloy particles. Accordingly, a highest efficiency of $18.89\%$ was demonstrated.[108b]
## 5.2.6. Mobility of HTL and Perovskite/HTL Interface Energy Level Alignment
Dong and co-workers reported that the replacement of poly3-hexylthiophene (P3HT) with diketopyrrolopyrrole-based copolymer (P) in ETL-free PSC $\mathrm{ITO/MAPbI_{3}/H T L/M o O_{3}/A g}$ can boost the device efficiency from $6.62\%$ to $10.80\%$ , which is ascribed to the higher carrier mobility, higher matched energy level, and lower interfacial charge recombination.[83] For the first time, the effect of the property of the HTL on the performance of the ETL-free PSCs was emphasized. By n-type doping of perovskite, Huang et  al. reported ETL-free PSCs with optimized interface energy level alignment, enhanced built-in electric field thus reduced interfacial charge recombination at both interfaces (Figure  10e,f).[54] The resulted ETL-free device with a Schottky/p–n cascade heterojunction presents a much higher efficiency than that of the control device with pristine perovskite.
## 5.3. Further Investigations Regarding Device Physics from Device Simulation
In spite of the development of ETL-free devices, little work has been conducted on the device simulation of ETL-free PSCs so far. However, device simulation is of great importance for the understanding of device physics and optimum design for further device performance enhancement. Device simulation promotes the design of the novel solar cell and simultaneously can be useful for exploring the fundamental physics of photovoltaic device. In order to explore the feasible direction of improving device performance of the ETL-free PSCs, Huang et  al. performed device modeling of ETL-free PSCs by systematically simulating the effects of material and device parameters on the final device performance.[91] They found that the FTO/perovskite interface defect density significantly influence the performance of the device. Also, the FTO/perovskite interface quality has more significant impact on the device performance than the perovskite/ HTL interface, suggesting that more attention should be paid to the front FTO/perovskite interface to further enhance the performance of ETL-free PSCs. With this aim, efficient strategy to passivate the interface defect density to the order of $10^{15}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ is necessary and urgently needed. With the optimized parameters, a highest PCE of $19.16\%$ was demonstrated, pointing out the great potential of ETL-free PSCs in term of device performance enhancement. After their work, Yu et  al. first boosted the PCE up to $18.20\%$ via solvent engineering and then further demonstrated a highest PCE of $19.20\%$ via electrochemical etching FTO electrode, which is a milestone for ETL-free PSCs.[93,102] Under the drift–diffusion equation framework, Choy and co-workers have explored the way to approach the efficiency limit and found that the PSC performance cannot be significantly improved by only inserting one blocking layer.[137] The PSC efficiency is limited by the surface recombination at the other contact without the blocking layer, which has a large surface recombination. In conclusion, the reduction of the surface carriers recombination at both the cathode and anode is of equal importance. Li and coworkers investigated the effects of doping type/concentration/ thickness of perovskite film on the photovoltaic response of the ETL-free PSCs in terms of the band bending, the built-in electrical field, and the surface/bulk recombination.[134] They found that decreasing the work function of FTO from 4.6 to $4.0\:\mathrm{eV}$ can decrease the FTO/perovskite interface recombination and greatly improve the device performance. With optimized parameters of n-type doping concentration of $1\times10^{17}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ , the PCE can be promoted to be $18.5\%$ when a Schottky contact is formed at the FTO/perovskite interface. Unlike traditional $\mathtt{p-i-n}$ structure Si based solar cells (for example, a-Si:H solar cell), inhomogeneous built-in field of can be formed in PSCs, resulting in different band structure. When the perovskite absorb layer is sandwiched between n- and p-type CTL, two active junctions immediately form at the ETL or FTO and the HTL sides. Baig et al. conducted numerical analysis of a novel $\mathrm{FTO/n–MAPbI_{3}/p–M A P b I_{3}/p}$ - $\mathrm{MAPb}\mathrm{Br}_{3}$ PSC via device simulation.[138] Because $\mathrm{MAPbI}_{3}$ was found to be self-doped between p-type and n-type by changing the ratio MAI and $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ , n-type $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ was used as alternate ETL without using extra ETL such as $\mathrm{SnO}_{2}$ and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ . PCE of $26.14\%$ was revealed with absorber thickness of $300\ \mathrm{nm}$ and n-type doping concentration of $6\times10^{16}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ . Wang and co-workers proposed a universal and simple method to analyze $J{-}V$ curves of planar PSCs by constructing a double p–n junctions equivalent circuit to fit $J{-}V$ curves of p–i–n planar PSCs and ETL-free PSCs, such a method can theoretically solves the dilemma that the diode ideal factor being larger than 2.[139] The new method focuses on the relationship between the diode ideal factor and the carrier recombination induced by interface defects, helping one reveal the operating mechanism and improve the device performance of ETL-free PSCs.
## 6. Stability, Hysteresis, and Recyclability
## 6.1. Stability
Stability is of vital importance for PSCs to be commercialized, without the UV light photocatalytic degradation effect of metal oxide based ETL (such as $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}\mathrm{,}$ ), the ETL-free devices are expected to be more stable. Many groups reported ETLfree PSCs that have higher durability from invasion of UV light and moisture compared with that of the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ inserted type. By removing the ZnO ETL, Dianyi et  al. observed an improvement in the stability of their devices (Figure S5a, Supporting Information).[73] In the work by Ke et al., the PCE of the nonencapsulated ETL-free PSC was about $70\%$ of its initial value after $500\mathrm{~h~}$ of storage in atmosphere with $20\%$ humidity and at room temperature under dark (Figure S5b, Supporting Information).[78] In the work by Cui et  al., the unsealed PSC stored in ambient air $45\mathrm{-}65\%$ humidity, $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ ) present PCE about $20\%$ higher than its initial PCE even after 21 days later, possibly due to oxidation of spiro-MeOTAD, by contrast, the efficiency of PSCs deposited on $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ drops about $20\%$ (Figure S5c, Supporting Information).[94] Yu et  al. found that the ETL-free PSCs had strong durability from attack by sunlight or moisture compared with device with $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ inserted as a $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ layer mainly destroys $\mathbf{MAPbI}_{3}$ at the interface by an UV light-induced trap-charge driven photocatalytic degradation effect (Figure S5d, Supporting Information).[93] The insertion of interfacial layer can also improve the stability of the device (Figure S5e–g, Supporting Information). Xiao and co-workers reported robust inverted ETL-free PSCs, the device with Ti interlayer maintained nearly $90\%$ and $77\%$ of the initial PCE after 15 and 40 days, respectively,[109] which represents a significant device stability improvement with metal cathode directly in contact with perovskite layer. In the work by Han et al., the PCE of the champion ETL-free device remains about $99.1\%$ of its initial value after $1000\mathrm{h}$ of storage ( $25\%$ humidity, $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ in the dark), illustrating the good environmental stability potential of ETL-free PSCs (Figure S5h,i, Supporting Information).[111]
As for the photostability, Pascual and co-workers demonstrated that introducing $\mathrm{C}_{70}$ into perovskite film significantly improves the photostability of ETL-free PSCs as the device suffered only about $10\%$ PCE reduction after $^3\mathrm{~h~}$ storage under continuous illumination.[97] In the work by Han et al., the photostability of the champion ETL-free devices without encapsulation was measured under continuous light soaking (1 sun) under ambient conditions $25\%$ humidity, $25~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ , after $192\mathrm{h}$ of irradiation, the device remains $590\%$ of its initial PCE, which is encouraging result for robust ETL-free PSC.[111] Table 5 summarizes the stability measurement parameters of ETL-free PSCs reported so far.
## 6.2. Hysteresis
Due to the defects existing in the bulk and/or at the surface of solution processed metal oxide semiconductor like $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL, the $J{-}V$ curves of devices present various degree of hysteresis effect. While for ETL-free device, ETL is absent and the contribution of ETL to $J{-}V$ hysteresis no longer exists. However, the $J{-}V$ curves of ETL-free PSCs still exhibit varying degree of hysteresis, possibly due to intrinsic ion migration in perovskite as widely reported. From Figure 18, the device structure, scanning rate, interface modification layer, and HTL specifically adopted all have effect on the hysteresis behavior. In the work by $\mathrm{Yu}$ et al., the extent of hysteresis (hysteresis index ${\approx}0.8$ ) in ETL-free PSC was much severe compared with that of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ -inserted PSC due to the much slower charge extraction rate at FTO/perovskite interface (Figure  18a).[93] While, for the mesoscopic PSC without ETL as reported by Han and co-workers, there were almost no substantial hysteresis effects observed as a very small hysteresis index of 0.014 was calculated, which is in accordance with the photoresponse behavior of the mesoscopic PSC with ETL (Figure  18b).[92] This suggests that the device structure has a remarkable effect on the hysteresis behavior of ETL-free PSCs. In their work by Yang and co-workers, the device ITO/ Cl-FAPbI3/spiro-MeOTAD/Au exhibits hysteresis effects that depends on the delay time (thus scanning rate) during scanning (Figure  18c).[82] While for the ETL-free PSC reported by Peng et  al. (Figure  18d), the $J{-}V$ curves with different scanning voltage steps and delay time showed little difference, possibly due to improved holes extraction at the perovskite/HTL interface that originated from the self doping of perovskite and optimized interface energy level alignment.[94] The interface modification layer and HTL adopted also have great influence on the hysteresis behavior of the $J{-}V$ curves. In the work by Park and co-workers (Figure  18e), the insertion of ultrathin PEIE not only improves the device efficiency but also decreases the hysteresis degree, possibly due to the passivation of interface defects and the optimization of the interface energy level alignment.[88] In the work by Chen et  al. (Figure  18f), enabled by the suitable energy level, higher carrier mobility, and lower interfacial charge recombination of PBDTTT-C HTL, the ETL-free devices show higher PCE than the devices with a P3HT HTL with much smaller degree of hysteresis.[96]
PSCs fabricated on recycled FTO/glass substrates are an attractive technology for scalable, sustainable, and environmentally friendly energy production.
## 6.3. Recyclability
To avoid environmental pollution by degraded device and achieve the reuse of resources, Huang et  al. demonstrated recyclable ETL-free PSCs based on reused FTO substrates that derived from the degraded devices (Figure 19a,b) and PCEs around $10\%$ after 2 times of recycling was achieved, which is close to that of the devices based on pristine FTO.[91] The solution processed PSCs can be easily separated and recycled into their major components using low-temperature wet processes including organic solvent washing, ultrasonic cleaning, and UV ozone treatment, the discarded substrates with high transparence (Figure  19c) can be readily reused for fabricating efficient ETL-free PSCs with well crystallized perovskite films Figure 19d,e. As the cost of FTO electrodes make up a large portion of the material cost of PSCs, recycling them can also reduce the fabrication cost. Yu et al., soon after, compared the recyclability of ETL-free and $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ ETL containing PSCs (Figure 19f,g).[93] They found that the performance of $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ based device was dramatically decreased after only one cycle, which was ascribed to the existence of residual $\mathrm{PbI}_{2}$ on the $\mathrm{TiO}_{2}$ surface, whereas the ETL-free device performance was equally maintained with that of the initial cell after four cycles (Figure 19f–h). Such efficient and readily recyclable
## 7. Summary and Outlook
The electronic bipolarity of perovskites to transport both holes and electrons makes it possible to fabricate efficient HTL-free devices and/or ETL-free devices. The elimination of the ETL not only simplifies the fabrication process of devices but also reduces the material cost and energy consumption, and paves the way to the large-scale manufacturing of PSCs. However, the development of ETL-free PSCs is hindered by their unsatisfactory performance currently. From Figure  20a, the values of $V_{\mathrm{oc}}$ , $J_{\mathrm{sc}}$ , FF, and PCE of ETL-free PSCs are mainly located at about $0.8–1.1~\mathrm{V},~16–23~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ , $60\mathrm{-}75\%$ , and $10\mathrm{-}16\%$ , respectively. All these values are close to those values reported of the traditional structured devices with ETL.
Within this review, we re-examine the recent progress in the simplification of the device structures of PSCs, mainly addressing the simple structured ETL-free PSCs. Despite the fact that the lack of ETL may result in poor interfacial contact and inferior device performance, researchers have made extensive efforts to solve these problems and improve the PCE of ETL-free devices in the past several years. From Figure 20b, the efficiency of ETL-free PSCs is much close to the efficiency of state of the art planar PSCs. Therefore, we believe that through more in-depth study, it is possible to fulfill ETL-free PSCs with much higher efficiency and stability.
## 7.2. Outlook on Future Directions
In addition to the primacy of perovskite films with high density and full coverage as revealed, the following issues should be addressed with further in-depth study.
## 7.1. Summary of Existing Problems
In spite of the great progress fulfilled, many issues remain unclear and to be solved: The working principle of the ETL-free PSCs is still controversial. There exist at least two points of view at present. One is that the built-in electric field associated with the TCO/perovskite MS junction serves as the driving force for electron extraction. The other is that the perovskite/HTL heterojunction serves as the driving force for charge extraction. At present, there is no clear evidence to exclude the built-in electric field established by prebias induced ion migration just as the case in the lateral devices. As for the device performance, there is still much room for efficiency improvement. The hysteresis effect needs to be completely eliminated in order to achieve robust and reliable device efficiency. Long-term stability of devices, especially on the month to year scale, requires further tracking studies. The flexibility potential of the ETL-free PSCs (with a reported highest efficiency of about $13\%$ presently) has hardly been tapped,[82] which is far behind that of the flexible devices with ETLs. Also, research on fabrication process of large area ETLfree PSCs awaits to be carried out.
## 7.2.1. Energy Level Alignment and Defect Passivation at the TCO/ Perovskite Interface
Other mechanisms must be adopted to fulfil the similar function of the ETL to achieve effective electron extraction and hole blocking. This involves the regulation of band bending at TCO/perovskite interface. Generally, there are two common ways to achieve this goal, that is conductive type control of perovskite film itself and effective work function control of TCO. Controllable doping of perovskite to regulate its polarity is still a great challenge and calls for substantial development, while the regulation of TCO work function seems to be easy to achieve by simple surface modification. In addition to the energy level matching problem, the TCO/perovskite interface defect is also an important factor restricting device performance. Previous simulation shows that the defect density at the interface must be reduced to the order of $10^{-15}~\mathrm{cm}^{-3}$ to ensure the a high efficiency exceeding $19\%$ .[91] Therefore, effective strategies for electronic defects passivation and structural defects elimination are urgently necessary.
## 7.2.2. Mobility, Energy Level, and Polarity Optimization of HTL
Apart from careful optimization of the front interface, the energy band structure at the perovskite/HTL interface has received little attention. To form highly efficient rectifying heterojunction with perovskite that ensures efficient hole extraction and transportation, the energy level and work function of HTL should also be finely controlled. The hole mobility of the widely adopted HTL material spiro-MeOTAD is much lower than that of perovskite, which may result in the accumulation of holes at the back interface. The hole mobility of spiroMeOTAD is also much lower than the electron mobility of perovskite and TCO, which can lead to unbalanced electron and hole transport at device level.[83,91] These two factors may lead to the accumulation and recombination of carriers, and hence the hysteresis effect.
## 7.2.3. Hysteresis, Stability, Flexibility, Scalability, and Sustainability of ETL-Free PSCs
As mentioned above the hysteresis, stability issues of ETL-free PSCs should be added to the research agenda as soon as possible. Although the efficiency of the ETL-free PSCs is getting much higher, which has approached or reached $20\%$ , the flexible devices based on the ETL-free structure have not made substantial progress. While, the efficiency record of flexible devices with ETL have reached $18.4\%$ .[55c-i] Considering the potential of ETL-free PSCs in flexible applications as mentioned, there is still much room for flexible ETL-free PSCs. For deployment of large-scale printing approaches in ETL-free PSCs manufacturing, high sensitivity of device performance to perovskite film coverage calls for large area film deposition process that can ensure dense film with full coverage. The possible environmental impacts of Pb based PSCs should be dealt with carefully. Meticulous end-of-life management to handle $\mathrm{\Pb}$ based PSCs is of significance to avoid Pb pollution. The proof-of-concept demonstration for facile disassembling of ETL-free PSCs shows great potential for recovery and reuse of the major components to facilitate readily recycling of degraded devices.
